  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 104 PM CDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\nRest of Today: Storm Chances This Evening\n\nPersistent cloud cover is forecast for the rest of today as low to \nmid level moisture continues to round the top of the H300 ridge of \nhigh pressure. As a result of the cloud cover, highs for this \nafternoon should be in the upper 70s to low 80s, so overall it will \nbe a very nice day even though the sun will not be out very much for \nmost areas. A shortwave trough, which is currently located over \nNebraska into South Dakota, will continue to move northeast and east \nthis afternoon into this evening. As the shortwave approaches the \nUpper Mississippi River Valley, a weak surface front should be \nmoving east across Minnesota into Wisconsin this evening. These \nfeatures combined with sufficient moisture throughout the column, \nshould lead to at least isolated to scattered showers and storms \nlater on today. Even though there are some chances (10-20 percent) \nfor a few pop-up showers this afternoon, the better chances for any \nrain will be as the front moves over the region this evening. Severe \nthunderstorms are not expected at this time. Even though moderate to \nheavy rain will be possible with the strongest cores, given PWAT \nvalues approaching 2 inches and long skinny CAPE profiles depicted \nby forecast soundings, the potential for flooding is low since the \nstorms should be moving fairly quickly. Any showers or storms that \ndo develop should dissipate or move off to the east very early \nSunday morning.\n\nSunday and Monday: Mild Temperatures and Drier Conditions\n\nHigh pressure across the southeast should decrease in strength on \nSunday into Monday and westerly flow is expected to prevail over the \nregion on both of these days. In the wake of the weak frontal \npassage on Saturday night into Sunday morning, drier air should \nsettle in over the area leading to dry conditions. Mostly clear \nskies on both of these days along with H850 temperatures in the mid \nteens Celsius should lead to seasonal temperatures to end the \nweekend and begin the next work week. The dry conditions will not \nlast long however, as moisture returns to the region on Monday \nevening. Lift provided by a fast moving shortwave may bring rain \nshowers and storms to the area going into Tuesday morning.\n\nRest of Next Week: Trending Cooler with More Chances for Rain\n\nWesterly to northwesterly upper level flow is forecast for the \nmiddle to later half of next week as troughing develops over eastern \nCanada into the northeastern CONUS. This trough will usher in a \ncooler air mass over the Upper Mississippi River Valley and cooler \ntemperatures in the 70s are expected through the next week. High \npressure is expected to remain mainly suppressed to the Desert \nSouthwest most of next week before potentially building back north \ntowards the Pacific Northwest by Friday. The best chance for rain \nnext week looks to be Tuesday night into Wednesday at this time. The \nlatest 15/13z NBM has up to a 30-40 percent chance to reach or \nexceed 1 inch of rain across the far southern portions of the CWA \nduring this time frame with higher chances (50-60 percent) across \ncentral Iowa. The area of the potential heaviest rainfall has \ntrended further south of the past 24 hours but given this is still 4 \ndays out there remains uncertainty in exactly where the best chances \nfor rain will fall. Given mainly westerly flow aloft, shortwaves \nembedded in this flow may lead to additional chances for showers and \nstorms beyond Tuesday, but timing differences leads to low \nconfidence at this time.",
